WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:07.870
Bernie Sanders has rocketed to the top of
the democratic presidential primary field

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:10.200
by proposing a massive expansion of government.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.160
Single payer health care, free public college
tuition, student loan forgiveness,

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.960
universal pre-k, and more.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.960
His plans could cost as much as 60 trillion
dollars over the next decade,

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.740
more than  doubling the federal budget.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.060
But more than any single policy, Sanders has
run on an idea,

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.400
Democratic Socialism, with Scandinavian countries as models.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.800
At times in his life however, he's also had
kind words for socialist revolutionairies,

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.320
and regimes that are far more authoritarian,
even though he has condemned

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.600
their harshest practices.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.900
Here to talk with me today about Sanders'
vision of Democratic Socialism,

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.300
and what it could mean in this country, is Jim Pethokoukis.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.300
He's the Dewitt Wallace fellow at the America
Enterprise Institute,

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.480
where he writes and edits the AEI Ideas blog.

